  "meta": {
    "title": "Vending machine: exactly 25¢",
    "blurb": "Nickels (n), dimes (d) and quarters (q), accepted when they add up to exactly 25 cents. The states are the running total, so the machine is a picture of every way to make change — and a coin too many falls into “overpaid”.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "q",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "a quarter"
      },
      {
        "w": "ddn",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "dnd",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "nnnnn",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "nothing paid"
      },
      {
        "w": "dd",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "20¢"
      },
      {
        "w": "qn",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "30¢"
      },
      {
        "w": "ddd",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "30¢"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "vending-machine",
        "real-world",
        "counting"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "A finite automaton can count, as long as it only needs to count to a fixed bound. Here the bound is 25¢: the state *is* the amount inserted so far, and any total past 25 is the same failure.\n\nEvery accepted word is a way of paying 25¢ in order — **q**, **ddn**, **dnd**, **ndd**, **nnnnn**, … There are nine of them — count the paths from 0¢ to 25¢ — and the machine recognises every one without listing any."
    }
  }
}
